## Log lines to the serial output, served by the log server component, the one
## holder of the PL011's serial channel (`doc/agentic/logging-design.md`).
##
## The lines travel on the interface's io-channel in transmit mode: the client
## owns the channel's data pages as 256-byte buffers, writes one line into a
## free buffer (without its line end) and puts a packet of one record naming
## it. The packet's value word is the line's header: its level, its timestamp,
## whether it was cut and how many lines the client dropped before it (the
## `log-server` crate encodes it). The server writes each line to the UART with
## a timestamp and level prefix, in the order the client put them, and hands
## the packet back: a packet back is a buffer free to reuse. Nobody waits on
## the channel's EventCount: the client takes packets back when it next logs.
## A packet with a record outside the data pages comes back unwritten.
##
## A client never waits for the log: with no buffer free, it drops the line and
## counts it. Prototype: edited in place.
local interface log_v1
{
    ## The io-channel the lines travel on: a control page and two data pages
    ## (32 lines in flight).
    channel 2;

    ## Every function receives the badge of the key it was called through.
    badged;

    ## Tell the server lines were put: it looks at every client's channel
    ## soon after. Never waits; the server also looks on its own every few
    ## milliseconds, so a line put without a `flush` is written too.
    flush();
}
